1. Diagnose the Actual Windows Environment
Inspect the repository, current terminal, and installed tools before making changes.

Run and record the results of commands appropriate to the environment:

Get-Location
python --version
py --list
where.exe python
where.exe pip
node --version
npm --version
git status --short
Check whether these files exist:

backend/manage.py

backend/requirements.txt

backend/.env.example

frontend/package.json

The existing MySQL configuration and migration files.

Determine the Python version required by the project and the version available on the system. Do not assume Python 3.12 is installed.

If Python or pip is unavailable, explain the exact issue and use an installed compatible Python interpreter if one exists. Do not install arbitrary Python versions or modify system settings without need.

2. Create or Repair the Django Virtual Environment
Use PowerShell commands appropriate to Windows.

From the project root, inspect whether backend/.venv or another virtual environment already exists. Reuse it if it is compatible; otherwise create a project-local environment without deleting existing project files.

Example, only if Python is installed and compatible:

cd backend
py -m venv .venv
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pip install --upgrade pip
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pip install -r requirements.txt
If py is unavailable, use the verified path to the installed compatible Python interpreter.

Handle installation failures by examining the actual error, Python version constraints, package compatibility, network availability, and compiler requirements. Do not randomly upgrade or downgrade dependencies.

Do not create a virtual environment inside a tracked production source directory if the repository has an established environment convention. Ensure virtual environments, .env files, database files, and generated secrets are excluded from Git.

3. Configure MySQL Safely
Inspect the project's Django database settings and environment variable names. Determine whether the application requires MySQL or already supports another database configuration.

On Windows, check for MySQL using available commands and Windows services, for example:

Get-Service *mysql*
Get-Command mysql -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
If MySQL is installed but stopped, identify the correct service and determine whether starting it is authorized and safe.

If MySQL is not installed or is unavailable, report the exact blocker and provide the appropriate setup instructions. Do not assume administrator permissions.

Validate database host, port, database name, user, and password configuration without printing secret values.

Do not:

Reset or recreate an existing database.

Drop tables or truncate data.

Change production credentials automatically.

Run destructive migrations.

Use real production data for automated tests.

Run seed scripts blindly.

If the project supports a dedicated test database, configure and use it for automated tests. Use an alternate database only if the existing project explicitly supports it and compatibility is verified.

4. Run Django Checks and Fix Actual Errors
Once dependencies and database configuration are ready, run the following from backend using the verified virtual environment:

.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe manage.py check
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe manage.py makemigrations --check --dry-run
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe manage.py migrate --check
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe manage.py test
If a command is incompatible with the project's installed Django version, determine the correct equivalent and document it.

For every failure:

Capture the relevant error.

Identify the underlying cause.

Fix the smallest appropriate code or configuration issue.

Rerun the failed check.

Inspect migration files before applying any migrations. Apply migrations only after confirming the target database and reviewing their impact.

Do not generate unnecessary migrations or alter legitimate existing data just to make checks pass.

Run manage.py check --deploy only after configuring appropriate production settings. Distinguish genuine deployment warnings from errors that require remediation.

5. Verify Real API Integration
Inspect Django URL configuration, views, serializers, models, permissions, and frontend API service configuration.

Verify the actual API endpoints used by:

Registration, login, logout, and token refresh.

Student lists and dashboards.

Reports and report reviews.

Internship opportunities, applications, and completion approvals.

Documents, verification, and secure downloads.

Skill assessments.

Community messages and announcements.

Coordinator management.

Notifications and analytics.

Check pagination, filters, response shapes, HTTP status codes, authentication headers, and role permissions.

Fix broken endpoints and integration errors in the existing implementation. Do not replace failed API requests with mock data or fabricated fallback records.

6. Preserve Real Student Registration and Remove Dummy Data
Maintain the requirement that only actual registered students and real database records appear in the application.

Registration must create a genuine database account.

Duplicate and invalid emails must be rejected.

The established secure role-assignment rules must remain intact.

Real students must appear in authorized lists after successful registration.

Empty lists must show genuine empty states.

Student actions must persist to the database.

Community messages must use authenticated sender identities.

Existing legitimate Admin, Coordinator, and Student records must be preserved.

Inspect all seed scripts and automatic sample-data creation. Never execute a script that may create fabricated users in the live database.

Delete only records positively verified as dummy/test records. Preserve ambiguous records for review. Do not delete users merely because their names match names previously used in sample data.

7. Verify Authentication and Permissions
Use a dedicated test environment to verify:

Valid and invalid login.

Registration and duplicate-email handling.

Token refresh and logout.

Role-based access for STUDENT, COORDINATOR, and ADMIN.

Object-level access to reports, applications, documents, and other private records.

Community sender identity and message permissions.

Rejection of unauthorized modifications.

Fix backend authorization issues rather than relying only on frontend route guards.

Do not introduce hardcoded credentials, shared PINs, or default production passwords.

8. Verify the Frontend Without Changing Forms or Design
From frontend, inspect package.json and run the existing project commands:

npm run build
npm run lint
Run existing automated tests if available.

Fix confirmed build failures, broken API configuration, runtime errors, and deployment blockers. Address lint warnings safely where feasible.

Preserve all existing forms and design. Do not change form fields, labels, validation rules, layouts, or styling unless a verified critical defect makes a minimal change unavoidable.

Confirm that the production build uses the correct backend API URL and contains no secrets.

9. Prepare Production Configuration
Inspect and prepare:

Django production settings.

Environment-based secret management.

DEBUG=False for production.

Appropriate ALLOWED_HOSTS.

HTTPS, secure cookies, CSRF, and CORS settings.

MySQL connectivity and migration procedure.

Static file serving and private document storage.

Authorized document downloads and upload validation.

Production frontend API URL.

Service startup and deployment instructions.

Logging and safe error responses.

Do not hardcode production secrets or expose them in Git, reports, logs, or frontend bundles.

Do not assume a hosting provider, domain, database host, or deployment credentials that have not been supplied.

10. Run the Application and Test Real Workflows
If the environment supports it, start the backend and frontend locally and verify the actual integration.

Test:

A student registers and logs in.

The registered student appears in the authorized student list.

The student submits a report or internship application.

The relevant Coordinator/Admin can view and process the record.

The student sees the updated status.

Document upload and authorized download work.

Community messages persist and show the correct sender.

Refreshing pages does not duplicate or recreate records.

Empty datasets do not show dummy students.

Unauthorized access is rejected.

Use a dedicated test database and controlled test accounts. Never run destructive tests against production.

If browser automation is unavailable, run API-level tests and provide the remaining manual verification steps.

11. Deployment and Final Report
Perform deployment preparation and actual deployment only where the required access and configuration are available.

Do not claim the website is deployed unless deployment has succeeded and the deployed application has been verified.

Update FINAL_FIX_REPORT.md with:

Root causes of the original environment blockers.

Environment setup and fixes performed.

Files actually modified.

Django check and migration results.

Frontend build and lint results.

API and permission test results.

End-to-end test results.

Production configuration status.

Exact remaining blockers and commands needed to resolve them.

Use only honest statuses: PASS, FAIL, NOT TESTED, and BLOCKED.

Start by inspecting the actual Windows environment and repository. Fix the real blockers instead of merely repeating the setup instructions. Continue through backend verification, frontend integration, and deployment preparation. Preserve the existing design, forms, legitimate database records, and working functionality.

